<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 3.2 - Bases and Coordinate Systems - Part 2</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Recall: if <m>\bcal = \{\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \ldots, \vvec_n\}</m> is a
            basis for <m>\real^n</m>, then if
            <md>
                \xvec = c_1\vvec_1+c_2\vvec_2 + \cdots + c_n\vvec_n,
            </md>
            then
            <md>
                \coords{\xvec}{\bcal} = \left[\begin{array}{c}{c_1}\\{c_2}\\ \vdots \\ c_n\end{array}\right].
            </md>
            That is, <m>\xvec</m> is the normal vector in standard form, and
            <m>\{\xvec\}_{\bcal}</m> is the vector of coefficients (weights) of
            the linear combination of the vectors in the basis <m>\bcal</m> that
            equals <m>\xvec</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\bcal</m> is the basis for <m>\real^2</m>
                consisting of the vectors <m>\vvec_1=\twovec21</m> and
                <m>\vvec_2=\twovec12</m>.
                Let's form the matrix
                <md>
                    P_{\bcal}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}\vvec_1 \amp \vvec_2 \\ \end{array}\right]
  =\mattwo2112.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why <m>P_{\bcal}\coords{\xvec}{\bcal} = \xvec</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    This is because <m>\mathcal{B}</m> is a basis and therefore
                    <m>\xvec</m> can be written as a linear combination of the
                    basis vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                    The coefficients for this linear combination are in
                    <m>\coords{\xvec}{\bcal}</m>.
                    When we perform the matrix vector multiplication
                    <m>P_{\bcal}\coords{\xvec}{\bcal} = c_1\vvec_1 + c_2\vvec_2 = \xvec</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a matrix <m>B</m> such that
                    <m>B\xvec = \coords{\xvec}{\bcal}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>B</m> is the inverse of <m>P_{\bcal}</m>!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.7in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Computers represent colors using a <em>color
    model</em>.
                The simplest is the <m>RGB</m> color model, which represents
                colors as a 3-dimensional vector <m>\threevec RGB</m> describing
                how much red <m>R</m>, green <m>G</m>, and blue <m>B</m> to mix
                together to create a color.
                These quantities are represented internally with 8 bytes so, in
                practice, the range of values is <m>0</m> to <m>255</m> for each
                color, <m>R</m>, <m>G</m>, and <m>B</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                A second color model is <m>YC_bC_r</m>, which is known as the
                <em>luminance-chrominance</em> color model.
                We introduce the basis <m>\bcal</m> consisting of vectors
                <md>
                    \vvec_1 = \threevec 111, 
  \vvec_2 = \threevec 0{-0.34413}{1.77200},
  \vvec_3 = \threevec {1.40200}{-0.71414}0,
                </md>
                and define
                <md>
                    \coords{\threevec RGB}{\bcal} = \threevec Y{C_b}{C_r}
                </md>
                The quantity <m>Y</m> is called the <em>luminance</em> and
                measures the brightness of the color, <m>C_b</m> is the blue
                chrominance and measures how much blue is mixed in, and
                <m>C_r</m> is the red chrominance and measures how much red is
                mixed in.
                The ranges of values are
                <md>
                    \begin{aligned}
    0 \amp \leq Y \leq 255 \\
    -127.5 \amp \leq C_b \leq 127.5 \\
    -127.5 \amp \leq C_r \leq 127.5 \\
  \end{aligned}
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                Go to <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Jc"/> where you will find
                some figures to experiment with color models.
                Remember that <m>R</m>, <m>G</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>Y</m> run
                between 0 and 255 while <m>C_b</m> and <m>C_r</m> run between
                -127.5 and 127.5.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when <m>G=0</m> and <m>B=0</m> (pushed all the
                    way to the left) and <m>R</m> is allowed to vary?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    This gives different shades of red!
                    This is based on how much black is added to the red.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when <m>R=0</m> and <m>G=0</m> (pushed all the
                    way to the left) and <m>B</m> is allowed to vary?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    This gives different shades of blue!
                    This is based on how much black is added to the blue.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What vector represents black in the <m>RGB</m> color model?
                    What vector represents white?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>\threevec{0}{0}{0}</m> is black.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>\threevec{255}{255}{255}</m> is white.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.75in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when <m>C_b=0</m> and <m>C_r=0</m> (kept in the
                    center) and <m>Y</m> is allowed to vary?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    The color changes along a spectrum of black, grey, and
                    white.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens when <m>Y=0</m> (pushed left) and <m>C_r=0</m>
                    (kept in the center) and <m>C_b</m> is allowed to increase
                    from 0 to 127.5?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    It starts at black and moves to full blue.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What vector represents black in the <m>YC_bC_r</m> color
                    model?
                    What vector represents white?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <m>\threevec{0}{0}{0}</m> is black.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>\threevec{255}{0}{0}</m> is white.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's find matrices that convert between these 2 bases for
                colors.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix <m>P_{\bcal}</m> that converts
                    <m>\threevec
  Y{C_b}{C_r}</m> into <m>\threevec RGB</m>
                    (that is
                    <m>P_{\bcal}\threevec{Y}{C_b}{C_r}=\threevec RGB</m> ).
                    You may want to give this a name in Sage; that is,
                    <c>Pb = matrix(...)</c>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        P_{\mathcal{B}} = [ \vvec_1 \,\, \vvec_2 \,\, \vvec_3 ] 
                    = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    1 \amp 0 \amp 1.40200 \\
                    1 \amp -0.34413 \amp -0.71414 \\
                    1 \amp 1.77200 \amp 0 \\
                    \end{array}\right].
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix that converts <m>\threevec RGB</m> into
                    <m>YC_bC_r</m>.
                    You may also want to give this a name in Sage.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1} 
                    = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    0.29900 \amp 0.58700 \amp 0.11400 \\
                    -0.16874 \amp -0.33126 \amp 0.50000 \\
                    0.50000 \amp -0.41869 \amp -0.08131 \\
                    \end{array}\right].
                    </md>
                    Note that these values are rounded to the 5 digit after the
                    decimal.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the <m>YC_bC_r</m> coordinates for the following colors
                    and use the diagrams to check that the two representations
                    agree.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        \threevec RGB = \threevec{255}00, 
  \threevec RGB = \threevec0{255}0, 
  \threevec RGB = \threevec{255}{255}{255}
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                       \threevec{Y}{C_b}{C_r} \amp = \coords{\threevec{255}00}{\mathcal{B}} \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\threevec{255}00 \amp = \threevec{76.25}{-43.03}{127.50}.
                        </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                       \threevec{Y}{C_b}{C_r} \amp = \coords{\threevec{0}{255}0}{\mathcal{B}} \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\threevec0{255}0 \amp = \threevec{149.69}{-84.47}{-106.77}.
                        </mrow>
                        <mrow>
\threevec{Y}{C_b}{C_r} \amp = \coords{\threevec{255}{255}{255}}{\mathcal{B}} \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}}^{-1}\threevec{255}{255}{255} \amp = \threevec{255}{0}{0}.
  </mrow>
                    </md>
                    Note that <m>-1.42108547152020e^{-14}</m> is numerically
                    equivalent to zero in this case.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the <m>RGB</m> coordinates for the following colors and
                    use the diagrams to check that the two representations
                    agree.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        \threevec Y{C_b}{C_r} = \threevec {128}00, 
  \threevec Y{C_b}{C_r} = \threevec {128}{60}0, 
  \threevec Y{C_b}{C_r} = \threevec {128}0{60}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                       \threevec RGB \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}} \threevec {128}00 = \amp \threevec{128}{128}{128}.
                        </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                       \threevec RGB \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}} \threevec {128}{60}0 = \amp \threevec{128}{107.3522}{234.32}.
                        </mrow>
                        <mrow>
\threevec RGB \amp = P_{\mathcal{B}} \threevec{128}0{60} = \amp \threevec{212.12}{85.1516}{128}.
  </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an expression for the luminance <m>Y</m> as it depends
                    on <m>R</m>, <m>G</m>, and <m>B</m>.
                    Explain how the luminance represents the brightness of the
                    color.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p/>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write an expression for the blue chrominance <m>C_b</m> in
                    terms of <m>R</m>, <m>G</m>, and <m>B</m>.
                    Explain how the blue chrominance measures the amount of blue
                    in the color.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="18-solutions">
                <p/>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions (at least for 2
                and 3 variables):
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Why might working in another basis be useful?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we convert from a vector in a non-standard basis
                    <m>\mathcal{B}</m> to its standard basis coordinates
                    algebraically?
                    (That is, <m>\{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m> to <m>\xvec</m>.)
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we convert from a vector in a standard basis to its
                    coordinates in another basis algebraically?
                    (That is, <m>\xvec</m> to <m>\{\xvec\}_{\mathcal{B}}</m>.)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>